    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0a880fef-2ac3-59b2-a00b-f5a6c3978166",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and zero.\n\n- $9.1=\\frac{91}{10}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $\\frac{91}{10}$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\sqrt{42}$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $0$: Zero is included by the subscript $0$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-\\frac{19}{5}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $9.1$; $0$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
