      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive real numbers and zero.\n\n- $\\frac{16}{6}=\\frac{8}{3}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\frac{7}{9}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\frac{33}{6}=\\frac{11}{2}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\frac{11}{6}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $\\frac{16}{6}$; $\\frac{7}{9}$; $\\frac{33}{6}$; $\\frac{11}{6}$.",
