      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$ is the set of all negative real numbers and zero.\n\n- $\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- $-\\frac{35}{4}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- $\\frac{26}{2}=13$: It is positive, and it is an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- $\\pi$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $-\\frac{35}{4}$.",
