      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and zero.\n\n- $2\\sqrt{6}$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $7.4=\\frac{37}{5}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $\\frac{37}{5}$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\sqrt{37}$: It is positive, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $0$: Zero is included by the subscript $0$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $7.4$; $0$.",
