      ":question/id": "b5d18db4-5c1b-5b72-9bd5-8054c2ddd8e8",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive real numbers and zero.\n\n- $-\\frac{6}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $\\frac{33}{3}=11$: It is positive, and it is an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $8.4=\\frac{42}{5}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $\\frac{42}{5}$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}=-2$: It is negative, and it is an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $\\frac{33}{3}$; $8.4$.",
