    "question": {
      ":question/id": "582e09b0-cc6e-5211-95d4-a011fdcd43e1",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $\\frac{15}{6}=\\frac{5}{2}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{36}=-6$: It is negative, and it is an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- $-1$: It is negative, and it is an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- $-5.1=-\\frac{51}{10}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $-\\frac{51}{10}$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $-\\sqrt{36}$; $-1$; $-5.1$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
