      ":question/id": "29edcd34-d9ea-5174-b268-1467017bb4c8",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$ is the set of all negative real numbers and zero.\n\n- $\\frac{6}{5}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- $-4.6=-\\frac{23}{5}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $-\\frac{23}{5}$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- $35$: It is positive, and it is an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- $4.7=\\frac{47}{10}$: It is positive, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $\\frac{47}{10}$. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $-4.6$.",
